# How an ABI fixture's `c-args:` are spelled for a target whose linker does
# not take the GNU spelling the fixture is written in.
#
# A fixture's `c-args:` are written once, as GCC and GNU ld (and LLVM's lld,
# which takes the same options) read them.  A line here says how TARGET's
# lane passes one of them instead:
#
#   TARGET  WRITTEN  PASSED
#
# WRITTEN ends in NAME, which matches the rest of the argument, the symbol
# it names; PASSED is the argument TARGET's lane passes, with NAME replaced
# by that symbol wherever it stands.  An argument no line matches is passed
# as written.  The test program reads this file through
# Landin.Testing.Fixtures and every Python lane through
# compiler/tests/fixture_meta.py, so the Darwin verdict of a fixture is the
# same whichever runner gives it; compiler/tests/fixture-meta.cases holds
# both readers to it.

# Apple's ld64 has no --wrap.  Its -alias makes the C name an alias of the
# wrapper, so the program's calls reach the wrapper as GNU ld's --wrap
# sends them; Mach-O spells every C symbol with a leading underscore.
darwin-arm64  -Wl,--wrap=NAME  -Wl,-alias,___wrap_NAME,_NAME
